					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/comparing-realities" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2503657); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2503657&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609254254" alt="Comparing Realities" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2503657)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/comparing-realities" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2503657); return false;" >Comparing Realities</a></span> <span class="odate time_1482611479 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">24 Dec 2016 20:31</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2714763">
									

<p>I see where you're coming from; as you said, the focus of this article is on the people involved and their exploration, not on the SCP itself. But I just feel that the magic of an SCP article is writing in such a way as to allow the reader to fill in the terrifying blanks themselves. My contention isn't that the presentation is bad. The presentation is what makes it good, in fact. I'm saying that the core concept of this article wouldn't work unless it was presented in the exact way you did it, <em>but</em>, it's also in the wrong format for me to appreciate the presentation, too. There aren't really any blanks for me to fill in, because the two survivors verbally expressed that &quot;oh crap&quot; moment before I got around to doing it myself. I felt kind of cheated. For that reason, I feel this would have worked far better as a tale, where I walk in with a different set of expectations.</p>

							</div>

								
